packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:1:Executes a given bash command in a persistent shell session with optional timeout, ensuring proper handling and security measures.
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:5:All commands run in ${directory} by default. Use the `workdir` parameter if you need to run a command in a different directory. AVOID using `cd <directory> && <command>` patterns - use `workdir` instead.
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:7:IMPORTANT: This tool is for terminal operations like git, npm, docker, etc. DO NOT use it for file operations (reading, writing, editing, searching, finding files) - use the specialized tools for this instead.
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:9:Before executing the command, please follow these steps:
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:12:   - If the command will create new directories or files, first use `ls` to verify the parent directory exists and is the correct location
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:22:   - After ensuring proper quoting, execute the command.
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:23:   - Capture the output of the command.
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:26:  - The command argument is required.
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:27:  - You can specify an optional timeout in milliseconds. If not specified, commands will time out after 120000ms (2 minutes).
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:28:  - It is very helpful if you write a clear, concise description of what this command does in 5-10 words.
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:29:  - If the output exceeds ${maxLines} lines or ${maxBytes} bytes, it will be truncated and the full output will be written to a file. You can use Read with offset/limit to read specific sections or Grep to search the full content. Do NOT use `head`, `tail`, or other truncation commands to limit output; the full output will already be captured to a file for more precise searching.
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:31:  - Avoid using Bash with the `find`, `grep`, `cat`, `head`, `tail`, `sed`, `awk`, or `echo` commands, unless explicitly instructed or when these commands are truly necessary for the task. Instead, always prefer using the dedicated tools for these commands:
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:38:  - When issuing multiple commands:
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:39:    - If the commands are independent and can run in parallel, make multiple Bash tool calls in a single message. For example, if you need to run "git status" and "git diff", send a single message with two Bash tool calls in parallel.
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:41:    - Use ';' only when you need to run commands sequentially but don't care if earlier commands fail
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:42:    - DO NOT use newlines to separate commands (newlines are ok in quoted strings)
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:43:  - AVOID using `cd <directory> && <command>`. Use the `workdir` parameter to change directories instead.
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:45:    Use workdir="/foo/bar" with command: pytest tests
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:57:- NEVER run destructive/irreversible git commands (like push --force, hard reset, etc) unless the user explicitly requests them
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:68:1. You can call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested and all commands are likely to succeed, run multiple tool calls in parallel for optimal performance. run the following bash commands in parallel, each using the Bash tool:
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:69:  - Run a git status command to see all untracked files.
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:70:  - Run a git diff command to see both staged and unstaged changes that will be committed.
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:71:  - Run a git log command to see recent commit messages, so that you can follow this repository's commit message style.
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:77:3. You can call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested and all commands are likely to succeed, run multiple tool calls in parallel for optimal performance. run the following commands:
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:85:- NEVER run additional commands to read or explore code, besides git bash commands
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:86:- NEVER use the TodoWrite or Task tools
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:88:- IMPORTANT: Never use git commands with the -i flag (like git rebase -i or git add -i) since they require interactive input which is not supported.
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:92:Use the gh command via the Bash tool for ALL GitHub-related tasks including working with issues, pull requests, checks, and releases. If given a GitHub URL use the gh command to get the information needed.
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:96:1. You can call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested and all commands are likely to succeed, run multiple tool calls in parallel for optimal performance. run the following bash commands in parallel using the Bash tool, in order to understand the current state of the branch since it diverged from the main branch:
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:97:   - Run a git status command to see all untracked files
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:98:   - Run a git diff command to see both staged and unstaged changes that will be committed
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:100:   - Run a git log command and `git diff [base-branch]...HEAD` to understand the full commit history for the current branch (from the time it diverged from the base branch)
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:102:3. You can call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested and all commands are likely to succeed, run multiple tool calls in parallel for optimal performance. run the following commands in parallel:
packages/opencode/src/tool/bash.txt:113:- DO NOT use the TodoWrite or Task tools
