     1	---
     2	title: Config
     3	description: Using the OpenCode JSON config.
     4	---
     5	
     6	You can configure OpenCode using a JSON config file.
     7	
     8	---
     9	
    10	## Format
    11	
    12	OpenCode supports both **JSON** and **JSONC** (JSON with Comments) formats.
    13	
    14	```jsonc title="opencode.jsonc"
    15	{
    16	  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
    17	  "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5",
    18	  "autoupdate": true,
    19	  "server": {
    20	    "port": 4096,
    21	  },
    22	}
    23	```
    24	
    25	---
    26	
    27	## Locations
    28	
    29	You can place your config in a couple of different locations and they have a
    30	different order of precedence.
    31	
    32	:::note
    33	Configuration files are **merged together**, not replaced.
    34	:::
    35	
    36	Configuration files are merged together, not replaced. Settings from the following config locations are combined. Later configs override earlier ones only for conflicting keys. Non-conflicting settings from all configs are preserved.
    37	
    38	For example, if your global config sets `autoupdate: true` and your project config sets `model: "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5"`, the final configuration will include both settings.
    39	
    40	---
    41	
    42	### Precedence order
    43	
    44	Config sources are loaded in this order (later sources override earlier ones):
    45	
    46	1. **Remote config** (from `.well-known/opencode`) - organizational defaults
    47	2. **Global config** (`~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`) - user preferences
    48	3. **Custom config** (`OPENCODE_CONFIG` env var) - custom overrides
    49	4. **Project config** (`opencode.json` in project) - project-specific settings
    50	5. **`.opencode` directories** - agents, commands, plugins
    51	6. **Inline config** (`OPENCODE_CONFIG_CONTENT` env var) - runtime overrides
    52	
    53	This means project configs can override global defaults, and global configs can override remote organizational defaults.
    54	
    55	:::note
    56	The `.opencode` and `~/.config/opencode` directories use **plural names** for subdirectories: `agents/`, `commands/`, `modes/`, `plugins/`, `skills/`, `tools/`, and `themes/`. Singular names (e.g., `agent/`) are also supported for backwards compatibility.
    57	:::
    58	
    59	---
    60	
    61	### Remote
    62	
    63	Organizations can provide default configuration via the `.well-known/opencode` endpoint. This is fetched automatically when you authenticate with a provider that supports it.
    64	
    65	Remote config is loaded first, serving as the base layer. All other config sources (global, project) can override these defaults.
    66	
    67	For example, if your organization provides MCP servers that are disabled by default:
    68	
    69	```json title="Remote config from .well-known/opencode"
    70	{
    71	  "mcp": {
    72	    "jira": {
    73	      "type": "remote",
    74	      "url": "https://jira.example.com/mcp",
    75	      "enabled": false
    76	    }
    77	  }
    78	}
    79	```
    80	
    81	You can enable specific servers in your local config:
    82	
    83	```json title="opencode.json"
    84	{
    85	  "mcp": {
    86	    "jira": {
    87	      "type": "remote",
    88	      "url": "https://jira.example.com/mcp",
    89	      "enabled": true
    90	    }
    91	  }
    92	}
    93	```
    94	
    95	---
    96	
    97	### Global
    98	
    99	Place your global OpenCode config in `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`. Use global config for user-wide server/runtime preferences like providers, models, and permissions.
   100	
   101	For TUI-specific settings, use `~/.config/opencode/tui.json`.
   102	
   103	Global config overrides remote organizational defaults.
   104	
   105	---
   106	
   107	### Per project
   108	
   109	Add `opencode.json` in your project root. Project config has the highest precedence among standard config files - it overrides both global and remote configs.
   110	
   111	For project-specific TUI settings, add `tui.json` alongside it.
   112	
   113	:::tip
   114	Place project specific config in the root of your project.
   115	:::
   116	
   117	When OpenCode starts up, it looks for a config file in the current directory or traverse up to the nearest Git directory.
   118	
   119	This is also safe to be checked into Git and uses the same schema as the global one.
   120	
   121	---
   122	
   123	### Custom path
   124	
   125	Specify a custom config file path using the `OPENCODE_CONFIG` environment variable.
   126	
   127	```bash
   128	export OPENCODE_CONFIG=/path/to/my/custom-config.json
   129	opencode run "Hello world"
   130	```
   131	
   132	Custom config is loaded between global and project configs in the precedence order.
   133	
   134	---
   135	
   136	### Custom directory
   137	
   138	Specify a custom config directory using the `OPENCODE_CONFIG_DIR`
   139	environment variable. This directory will be searched for agents, commands,
   140	modes, and plugins just like the standard `.opencode` directory, and should
   141	follow the same structure.
   142	
   143	```bash
   144	export OPENCODE_CONFIG_DIR=/path/to/my/config-directory
   145	opencode run "Hello world"
   146	```
   147	
   148	The custom directory is loaded after the global config and `.opencode` directories, so it **can override** their settings.
   149	
   150	---
   151	
   152	## Schema
   153	
   154	The server/runtime config schema is defined in [**`opencode.ai/config.json`**](https://opencode.ai/config.json).
   155	
   156	TUI config uses [**`opencode.ai/tui.json`**](https://opencode.ai/tui.json).
   157	
   158	Your editor should be able to validate and autocomplete based on the schema.
   159	
   160	---
   161	
   162	### TUI
   163	
   164	Use a dedicated `tui.json` (or `tui.jsonc`) file for TUI-specific settings.
   165	
   166	```json title="tui.json"
   167	{
   168	  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/tui.json",
   169	  "scroll_speed": 3,
   170	  "scroll_acceleration": {
   171	    "enabled": true
   172	  },
   173	  "diff_style": "auto"
   174	}
   175	```
   176	
   177	Use `OPENCODE_TUI_CONFIG` to point to a custom TUI config file.
   178	
   179	Legacy `theme`, `keybinds`, and `tui` keys in `opencode.json` are deprecated and automatically migrated when possible.
   180	
   181	[Learn more about TUI configuration here](/docs/tui#configure).
   182	
   183	---
   184	
   185	### Server
   186	
   187	You can configure server settings for the `opencode serve` and `opencode web` commands through the `server` option.
   188	
   189	```json title="opencode.json"
   190	{
   191	  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
   192	  "server": {
   193	    "port": 4096,
   194	    "hostname": "0.0.0.0",
   195	    "mdns": true,
   196	    "mdnsDomain": "myproject.local",
   197	    "cors": ["http://localhost:5173"]
   198	  }
   199	}
   200	```
   201	
   202	Available options:
   203	
   204	- `port` - Port to listen on.
   205	- `hostname` - Hostname to listen on. When `mdns` is enabled and no hostname is set, defaults to `0.0.0.0`.
   206	- `mdns` - Enable mDNS service discovery. This allows other devices on the network to discover your OpenCode server.
   207	- `mdnsDomain` - Custom domain name for mDNS service. Defaults to `opencode.local`. Useful for running multiple instances on the same network.
   208	- `cors` - Additional origins to allow for CORS when using the HTTP server from a browser-based client. Values must be full origins (scheme + host + optional port), eg `https://app.example.com`.
   209	
   210	[Learn more about the server here](/docs/server).
   211	
   212	---
   213	
   214	### Tools
   215	
   216	You can manage the tools an LLM can use through the `tools` option.
   217	
   218	```json title="opencode.json"
   219	{
   220	  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
   221	  "tools": {
   222	    "write": false,
   223	    "bash": false
   224	  }
   225	}
   226	```
   227	
   228	[Learn more about tools here](/docs/tools).
   229	
   230	---
   231	
   232	### Models
   233	
   234	You can configure the providers and models you want to use in your OpenCode config through the `provider`, `model` and `small_model` options.
   235	
   236	```json title="opencode.json"
   237	{
   238	  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
   239	  "provider": {},
   240	  "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5",
   241	  "small_model": "anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5"
   242	}
   243	```
   244	
   245	The `small_model` option configures a separate model for lightweight tasks like title generation. By default, OpenCode tries to use a cheaper model if one is available from your provider, otherwise it falls back to your main model.
   246	
   247	Provider options can include `timeout`, `chunkTimeout`, and `setCacheKey`:
   248	
   249	```json title="opencode.json"
   250	{
   251	  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
   252	  "provider": {
   253	    "anthropic": {
   254	      "options": {
   255	        "timeout": 600000,
   256	        "chunkTimeout": 30000,
   257	        "setCacheKey": true
   258	      }
   259	    }
   260	  }

