     8	
     9	OpenCode uses the [AI SDK](https://ai-sdk.dev/) and [Models.dev](https://models.dev) to support **75+ LLM providers** and it supports running local models.
    10	
    11	To add a provider you need to:
    12	
    13	1. Add the API keys for the provider using the `/connect` command.
    14	2. Configure the provider in your OpenCode config.
    15	
    16	---
    17	
    18	### Credentials
    19	
    20	When you add a provider's API keys with the `/connect` command, they are stored
    21	in `~/.local/share/opencode/auth.json`.
    22	
    23	---
    24	
    25	### Config
    26	
    27	You can customize the providers through the `provider` section in your OpenCode
    28	config.
    29	
    30	---
    31	
    32	#### Base URL
    33	
    34	You can customize the base URL for any provider by setting the `baseURL` option. This is useful when using proxy services or custom endpoints.
    35	
    36	```json title="opencode.json" {6}
    37	{
    38	  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
    39	  "provider": {
    40	    "anthropic": {
    41	      "options": {
    42	        "baseURL": "https://api.anthropic.com/v1"
    43	      }
    44	    }
    45	  }
    46	}
    47	```
    48	
    49	---
    50	
    51	## OpenCode Zen
    52	
    53	OpenCode Zen is a list of models provided by the OpenCode team that have been
    54	tested and verified to work well with OpenCode. [Learn more](/docs/zen).
    55	
    56	:::tip
    57	If you are new, we recommend starting with OpenCode Zen.
    58	:::
    59	
    60	1. Run the `/connect` command in the TUI, select `OpenCode Zen`, and head to [opencode.ai/auth](https://opencode.ai/zen).
    61	
    62	   ```txt
    63	   /connect
    64	   ```
    65	
    66	2. Sign in, add your billing details, and copy your API key.
    67	
    68	3. Paste your API key.
    69	
    70	   ```txt
    71	   ┌ API key
    72	   │
    73	   │
    74	   └ enter
    75	   ```
    76	
    77	4. Run `/models` in the TUI to see the list of models we recommend.
    78	
    79	   ```txt
    80	   /models
    81	   ```
    82	
    83	It works like any other provider in OpenCode and is completely optional to use.
    84	
    85	---
    86	
    87	## OpenCode Go
    88	
    89	OpenCode Go is a low cost subscription plan that provides reliable access to popular open coding models provided by the OpenCode team that have been
    90	tested and verified to work well with OpenCode.
    91	
    92	1. Run the `/connect` command in the TUI, select `OpenCode Go`, and head to [opencode.ai/auth](https://opencode.ai/zen).
    93	
    94	   ```txt
    95	   /connect
    96	   ```
    97	
    98	2. Sign in, add your billing details, and copy your API key.
    99	
   100	3. Paste your API key.
   101	
   102	   ```txt
   103	   ┌ API key
   104	   │
   105	   │
   106	   └ enter
   107	   ```
   108	
   109	4. Run `/models` in the TUI to see the list of models we recommend.
   110	
   111	   ```txt
   112	   /models
   113	   ```
   114	
   115	It works like any other provider in OpenCode and is completely optional to use.
   116	
   117	---
   118	
   119	## Directory
   120	
   121	Let's look at some of the providers in detail. If you'd like to add a provider to the
   122	list, feel free to open a PR.
   123	
   124	:::note
   125	Don't see a provider here? Submit a PR.
   126	:::
   127	
   128	---
   129	
   130	### 302.AI
   131	
   132	1. Head over to the [302.AI console](https://302.ai/), create an account, and generate an API key.
   133	
   134	2. Run the `/connect` command and search for **302.AI**.
   135	
   136	   ```txt
   137	   /connect
   138	   ```
   139	
   140	3. Enter your 302.AI API key.
   141	
   142	   ```txt
   143	   ┌ API key
   144	   │
   145	   │
   146	   └ enter
   147	   ```
   148	
   149	4. Run the `/models` command to select a model.
   150	
   151	   ```txt
   152	   /models
   153	   ```
   154	
   155	---
   156	
   157	### Amazon Bedrock
   158	
   159	To use Amazon Bedrock with OpenCode:
   160	
   161	1. Head over to the **Model catalog** in the Amazon Bedrock console and request
   162	   access to the models you want.
   163	
   164	   :::tip
   165	   You need to have access to the model you want in Amazon Bedrock.
   166	   :::
   167	
   168	2. **Configure authentication** using one of the following methods:
   169	
   170	   ***
   171	
   172	   #### Environment Variables (Quick Start)
   173	
   174	   Set one of these environment variables while running opencode:
   175	
   176	   ```bash
   177	   # Option 1: Using AWS access keys
   178	   AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=XXX AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=YYY opencode
   179	
   180	   # Option 2: Using named AWS profile
   181	   AWS_PROFILE=my-profile opencode
   182	
   183	   # Option 3: Using Bedrock bearer token
   184	   AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK=XXX opencode
   185	   ```
   186	
   187	   Or add them to your bash profile:
   188	
   189	   ```bash title="~/.bash_profile"
   190	   export AWS_PROFILE=my-dev-profile
   191	   export AWS_REGION=us-east-1
   192	   ```
   193	
   194	   ***
   195	
   196	   #### Configuration File (Recommended)
   197	
   198	   For project-specific or persistent configuration, use `opencode.json`:
   199	
   200	   ```json title="opencode.json"
   201	   {
   202	     "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
   203	     "provider": {
   204	       "amazon-bedrock": {
   205	         "options": {
   206	           "region": "us-east-1",
   207	           "profile": "my-aws-profile"
   208	         }
   209	       }
   210	     }
   211	   }
   212	   ```
   213	
   214	   **Available options:**
   215	   - `region` - AWS region (e.g., `us-east-1`, `eu-west-1`)
   216	   - `profile` - AWS named profile from `~/.aws/credentials`
   217	   - `endpoint` - Custom endpoint URL for VPC endpoints (alias for generic `baseURL` option)
   218	
   219	   :::tip
   220	   Configuration file options take precedence over environment variables.

